1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,694
A Mercedes G 63 AMG 6x6 weighs

2
00:00:02,694 --> 00:00:05,119
about nine thousand pounds,

3
00:00:05,119 --> 00:00:08,172
stands on thirty-seven inch tyres,

4
00:00:08,172 --> 00:00:12,124
and was priced at three hundred seventy-nine

5
00:00:12,124 --> 00:00:14,280
thousand euros when new.

6
00:00:14,460 --> 00:00:16,538
Roughly one hundred were built.

7
00:00:16,718 --> 00:00:19,068
In this video we explain two things

8
00:00:19,068 --> 00:00:21,417
you probably did not know about it:

9
00:00:21,417 --> 00:00:23,364
what it really is underneath,

10
00:00:23,364 --> 00:00:25,983
and where its hardware went afterwards.

11
00:00:26,163 --> 00:00:28,729
Every figure comes from a named source.

12
00:00:29,079 --> 00:00:32,608
We used Wikipedia's pages on the 6x6

13
00:00:32,608 --> 00:00:34,471
and on the G-Class,

14
00:00:34,471 --> 00:00:36,726
Gear Patrol, The Drive,

15
00:00:36,726 --> 00:00:40,157
Road and Track via Motor Authority,

16
00:00:40,157 --> 00:00:43,196
CarAdvice via Driven Car Guide,

17
00:00:43,196 --> 00:00:45,843
MercedesBlog and Carscoops.

18
00:00:46,023 --> 00:00:47,969
They disagree in places,

19
00:00:47,969 --> 00:00:49,672
for example on power,

20
00:00:49,672 --> 00:00:50,969
price and dates.

21
00:00:51,149 --> 00:00:52,925
We show the disagreements.

22
00:00:53,105 --> 00:00:56,252
Our own maths is labelled as ours.

23
00:00:56,601 --> 00:00:57,600
Secret one.

24
00:00:57,780 --> 00:01:00,669
The G 63 AMG 6x6 was not

25
00:01:00,669 --> 00:01:03,678
designed as a luxury toy.

26
00:01:03,858 --> 00:01:06,439
Wikipedia says it is derived from a

27
00:01:06,439 --> 00:01:11,086
six-wheel-drive Geländewagen developed for the Australian Army,

28
00:01:11,086 --> 00:01:14,109
with development from two thousand seven.

29
00:01:14,289 --> 00:01:17,071
Gear Patrol says it is built on

30
00:01:17,071 --> 00:01:20,033
the military version of the G 320

31
00:01:20,033 --> 00:01:23,533
CDI, in partnership with Magna Steyr in

32
00:01:23,533 --> 00:01:24,251
Austria.

33
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:26,911
The sources disagree on the year.

34
00:01:27,091 --> 00:01:28,292
Road and Track,

35
00:01:28,292 --> 00:01:31,814
quoted by Motor Authority in November twenty

36
00:01:31,814 --> 00:01:35,256
twenty, says the army vehicle was developed

37
00:01:35,256 --> 00:01:37,897
in twenty eleven and based on the

38
00:01:37,897 --> 00:01:39,258
G 320 CDI diesel.

39
00:01:39,438 --> 00:01:42,074
Wikipedia says two thousand seven.

40
00:01:42,254 --> 00:01:44,599
We cannot settle it from our sources.

41
00:01:44,779 --> 00:01:46,163
By our arithmetic,

42
00:01:46,163 --> 00:01:48,854
the two dates are four years apart.

43
00:01:49,203 --> 00:01:52,040
The military version did not end when

44
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:53,650
the civilian one did.

45
00:01:53,830 --> 00:01:57,080
CarAdvice, in its report of the thirteenth

46
00:01:57,080 --> 00:01:58,937
of April twenty fifteen,

47
00:01:58,937 --> 00:02:02,419
says the Australian Army chassis is different

48
00:02:02,419 --> 00:02:05,359
from the AMG version and will continue

49
00:02:05,359 --> 00:02:06,520
to be produced.

50
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:09,687
So the civilian 6x6 was a spin-off

51
00:02:09,687 --> 00:02:11,708
from a working vehicle,

52
00:02:11,708 --> 00:02:13,817
not the other way round.

53
00:02:14,166 --> 00:02:17,413
Wikipedia says the 6x6 was introduced for

54
00:02:17,413 --> 00:02:21,214
the thirtieth anniversary of G-Wagen production.

55
00:02:21,394 --> 00:02:23,703
It adds that at launch it was

56
00:02:23,703 --> 00:02:27,366
the company's largest off-road vehicle and its

57
00:02:27,366 --> 00:02:30,631
second most expensive street-legal model,

58
00:02:30,631 --> 00:02:33,259
after the SLS AMG Electric Drive.

59
00:02:33,439 --> 00:02:36,307
So an army-derived truck was priced above

60
00:02:36,307 --> 00:02:38,896
nearly everything else Mercedes sold.

61
00:02:39,245 --> 00:02:40,314
Where was it built?

62
00:02:40,494 --> 00:02:43,782
Wikipedia says Magna Steyr in Graz,

63
00:02:43,782 --> 00:02:44,534
Austria.

64
00:02:44,714 --> 00:02:47,202
Gear Patrol says the work was done

65
00:02:47,202 --> 00:02:49,544
in partnership with Magna Steyr.

66
00:02:49,724 --> 00:02:53,814
Wikipedia's G-Class page mentions Magna Steyr only

67
00:02:53,814 --> 00:02:56,922
as a general assembler of the G-Class.

68
00:02:57,102 --> 00:02:59,963
Neither page gives details of how many

69
00:02:59,963 --> 00:03:02,373
workers or hours each unit took.

70
00:03:02,722 --> 00:03:04,777
Start with the drivetrain,

71
00:03:04,777 --> 00:03:05,962
from Wikipedia.

72
00:03:06,142 --> 00:03:09,117
It has six-wheel drive with portal axles

73
00:03:09,117 --> 00:03:11,796
similar to those on Unimog vehicles.

74
00:03:11,976 --> 00:03:14,663
The transfer case has a high ratio

75
00:03:14,663 --> 00:03:17,192
of zero point eight seven to one

76
00:03:17,192 --> 00:03:19,405
and a low ratio of two point

77
00:03:19,405 --> 00:03:20,590
one six to one.

78
00:03:20,770 --> 00:03:23,195
Power is nominally split thirty,

79
00:03:23,195 --> 00:03:26,453
forty and thirty percent between the front,

80
00:03:26,453 --> 00:03:28,119
middle and rear axles.

81
00:03:28,469 --> 00:03:29,771
By our arithmetic,

82
00:03:29,771 --> 00:03:30,927
a thirty, forty,

83
00:03:30,927 --> 00:03:33,963
thirty split of the five hundred sixty-one

84
00:03:33,963 --> 00:03:36,999
pound-feet of engine torque would be about

85
00:03:36,999 --> 00:03:38,733
one hundred sixty-eight,

86
00:03:38,733 --> 00:03:42,420
two hundred twenty-four and one hundred sixty-eight

87
00:03:42,420 --> 00:03:44,155
pound-feet at each axle.

88
00:03:44,335 --> 00:03:46,776
That ignores gearing and losses,

89
00:03:46,776 --> 00:03:48,912
so it only shows the idea of

90
00:03:48,912 --> 00:03:49,675
the split.

91
00:03:49,855 --> 00:03:52,364
Brabus, on its modified car,

92
00:03:52,364 --> 00:03:54,156
also lists a thirty,

93
00:03:54,156 --> 00:03:56,217
forty, thirty division.

94
00:03:56,567 --> 00:04:00,262
Five electronic differential locks can lock all

95
00:04:00,262 --> 00:04:02,384
six wheels, Wikipedia says.

96
00:04:02,564 --> 00:04:04,431
Gear Patrol puts it as up to

97
00:04:04,431 --> 00:04:07,231
one hundred percent lock-up across all six

98
00:04:07,231 --> 00:04:09,298
wheels, with low-range gearing.

99
00:04:09,478 --> 00:04:13,048
Road and Track repeats five differential locks

100
00:04:13,048 --> 00:04:15,920
and one hundred percent lockup on all

101
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:16,386
axles.

102
00:04:16,566 --> 00:04:18,870
Three outlets give the same claim,

103
00:04:18,870 --> 00:04:20,226
which is consistent,

104
00:04:20,226 --> 00:04:21,988
although none measured it.

105
00:04:22,338 --> 00:04:24,439
The tyre system is unusual.

106
00:04:24,619 --> 00:04:27,922
Wikipedia says an air compressor fills four

107
00:04:27,922 --> 00:04:31,685
